put), not from UAF.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"No crash primitive is triggerable from BUG-001. The swap-remove logic prevents any dereference of freed pointers.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"UAF write via edit_cookie is not triggerable because the swap-remove logic in eat_cookie prevents the freed pointer from remaining in any accessible msg[] slot. When idx < cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is overwritten by msg[cookie_num-1]. When idx == cookie_num-1 (self-assignment), the freed pointer stays but cookie_num-- makes it inaccessible (index == new_cookie_num fails strict less-than check). Dynamic verification with gdb confirmed this in 5+ test scenarios.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-002\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_READ\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"UAF read via read_cookie is not triggerable for the same reason as NP-001. The freed pointer is never in an accessible slot after eat_cookie returns.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-003\",\"candidate_type\":\"HEAP_OBJECT_CONTROL\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"Indirect UAF via tcache reuse is not possible because (1) glibc 2.39 calloc does NOT reuse tcache chunks - all tested allocations returned new chunks from top chunk, and (2) swap-remove overwrites the freed pointer in the accessible slot, so even if calloc reused the chunk, no aliasing would occur.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-004\",\"candidate_type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"The INFO_LEAK observed during testing (3 bytes of stale heap metadata via printf %s overread) is NOT from BUG-001. It is from calloc's incomplete clearing (chunksize - SIZE_SZ bytes instead of full usable size) combined with printf %s reading until NUL. This occurs on any read_cookie call after create_cookie with a size that fills the cleared area, regardless of whether eat_cookie was called. It is an independent issue, not a UAF consequence.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the stale pointer in msg[cookie_num] (old last slot after swap-remove) can be accessed via BUG-002 (signedness mismatch in index check)\",\"reason\":\"The swap-remove leaves a stale pointer at msg[cookie_num] (inaccessible via normal index check). BUG-002 allows large unsigned indices that pass the signed jl check. If a negative-signed index can compute an address landing on msg[cookie_num], the stale pointer could be accessed. This is a cross-BUG analysis that belongs to S4.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S4 should analyze whether BUG-001 + BUG-002 can be combined: use BUG-002 to access msg[cookie_num] (stale pointer from BUG-001's swap-remove), then edit/read through the stale pointer for UAF.\"}]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
